  "preamble": "/d/ becomes [ʒ] through palatalization before a yod: dj gives an affricate [dʒ], which then loses its stop part. Latin dj/di̯ became Old French [dʒ], later [ʒ] (diurnum > jour), and gave Portuguese [ʒ] (hodiē > hoje). Proto-Slavic *dj (*ď) became ž [ʒ] in Ukrainian (*meďa > межа 'border').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Spirantization, Deaffrication",
  "languageExamples": [
